<worksheet>
<title>Solving Quadratic Equations Using the Quadratic Formula</title>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solve quadratic equations using the quadratic formula.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Identify the three types of solutions for quadratic equations.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Develop a strategy for solving quadratic equations
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    The <term>standard form</term> of a quadratic equation is <m>y=ax^2 + bx + c</m> or <m>0=ax^2 + bx + c</m>. It is possible using algebra to write every quadratic equation
    in standard form. From this form, a formula can be derived that solves for <m>x</m>. More on where this formula comes from in the next lesson.
</p>
<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-quadratic-formula">
    <title>The Quadratic Formula</title>
    <p>
        Given a quadratic equation of the form <m>ax^2 + bx  +c = 0</m>
        <md>
            x = \frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}
        </md>
    </p>
</assemblage>
<example>
    <title>Solve using the Quadratic Formula</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the equation <m>4x^2-3x-5=0</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x = \frac{3\pm \sqrt{89}}{8} \approx 1.55, -0.8</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            <m>a = 4</m>, <m>b=-3</m> and <m>c=-5</m>. Inserting these values into quadratic formula gives:
            <md>
                x = \frac{-(-3)\pm\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4(4)(-5)}}{2(4)} = \frac{3\pm \sqrt{89}}{8}
            </md>
            We could leave the solution written like this in exact form or plug into a calculator to get the decimal approximations 1.55 and -0.8.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example>
    <title>Solve using the Quadratic Formula</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the equation <m>3x^2-x-8=0</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x = \frac{1\pm \sqrt{97}}{6} \approx 1.81, -1.47</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            <m>a = 3</m>, <m>b=-1</m> and <m>c=-8</m>. Inserting these values into quadratic formula gives:
            <md>
                x = \frac{-(-1)\pm\sqrt{(-1)^2 - 4(3)(-8)}}{2(3)} = \frac{1\pm \sqrt{97}}{6}
            </md>
            We could leave the solution written like this in exact form or plug into a calculator to get the decimal approximations 1.81 and -1.47.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<p>
    Sometimes some preliminary algebra steps are needed to put an equation in standard form before applying the quadratic formula.
</p>
<example>
    <title>Solve using the Quadratic Formula</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the equation <m>-3x+8 = 4x^2</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x = \frac{-3\pm \sqrt{137}}{8} \approx 1.09, -1.84</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            First subtract <m>-4x^2</m> from both sides of the equation to put into the standard form <m>-4x^2-3x+8=0</m>
            <m>a = -4</m>, <m>b=-3</m> and <m>c=8</m>. Inserting these values into quadratic formula gives:
            <md>
                x = \frac{-(-3)\pm\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4(-4)(8)}}{2(-4)} = \frac{-3\pm \sqrt{137}}{8}
            </md>
            We could leave the solution written like this in exact form or plug into a calculator to get the decimal approximations 1.09 and -1.84.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example>
    <title>Solve using the Quadratic Formula</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the equation <m>3x^2-5=x</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x = \frac{1\pm \sqrt{61}}{6}</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            First add <m>x</m> to both sides to put the quadratic equation in standard form. Then  <m>a = 3</m>, <m>b=-1</m> and <m>c=-5</m>. 
            Inserting these values into quadratic formula gives:
            <md>
                x = \frac{-(-1)\pm\sqrt{(-1)^2 - 4(3)(-5)}}{2(3)} = \frac{1\pm \sqrt{61}}{6}
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<p>
    So far we have only seen examples where the result under the square root is positive. But it is possible, that we could end up with a zero or a negative number under
    the square root. This leads to three types of solutions for a quadratic equation.
    <dl>
        <li>
            <title>Two Real Solutions</title>
            <p>
               If the number under the square root is positive, there will be two real solutions to the quadratic equation. For example <m>2x^2+9x-5</m> has the solution:
               <md>
                x = \frac{-9\pm\sqrt{9^2 - 4(2)(-5)}}{2(2)} = \frac{-9\pm\sqrt{131}}{4}
               </md>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>One Real (Repeated) Solution</title>
            <p>
               If the number under the square root is zero, the part after the <m>\pm</m> falls away, leaving only one solution. This is equivalent to factoring a quadratic
               into two identical factors. For example <m>4x^2+20x+25=0</m> has the solution:
               <md>
                x = \frac{-20\pm\sqrt{20^2 - 4(4)(25)}}{2(4)} = \frac{-20\pm\sqrt{0}}{8} =-\frac{5}{2}
               </md>
               Note that we can also factor this as a perfect square trinomial. <m>4x^2+20x+25 = (2x+5)(2x+5) = 0 \rightarrow x = -5/2</m>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Complex Conjugate Solutions</title>
            <p>
                If the number under the square root is negative, the part under the square root is an imaginary number since mathematicians define <m>\sqrt{-1} = i</m>. This
                leads to a pair of solutions that are called complex conjugates. For example <m>x^2+x+1=0</m> has the solution:
                <md>
                    x = \frac{-1\pm\sqrt{1^2-4(1)(1)}}{2(1)} = \frac{-1\pm i\sqrt{3}}{2}
                </md>
                Note that you can also get a complex conjugate pair from solving a quadratic equation using square roots. <m>x^2 = -4 \rightarrow x = \pm 2i</m>
            </p>
        </li>
    </dl>
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-quadratic-strategy">
    <title>A Strategy for Solving Quadratic Equations</title>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                Check to see if the equation consists of only a square term and constants. The square term may look like <m>(x-3)^2</m>. If it does, then use square roots to
                solve the equation.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                If the equation contains and linear term, like <m>8x</m>, use algebra to put the equation in standard form.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Check to see if the equation in standard form is easily factorable, then solve the equation by factoring.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                If all else fails, use the quadratic formula.
            </p>
        </li>
        </ol>  
    </p>
    
</assemblage>
<p>
    <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
</p>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Solve the following quadratic equations using the quadratic formula.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>4x^2-5x+1 = 0 </m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>2x^2+10x+11 = 0 </m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>3x^2+2x+9 = 0 </m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>x(x+6) = -4 </m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{3}{4}x^2+\frac{1}{2}x = \frac{3}{8} </m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>16x^2+8x+1 = 0 </m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
</worksheet>